    "narrative": "The Ghost Ride the WHIP lineup is anchored today by a trio of outfielders providing both elite efficiency and stolen base upside. **James Wood** (75.59) leads the charge in an optimal leadoff spot against Ginn, buoyed by a 0.437 xwOBA and a generous park factor. Close behind, **Mickey Moniak** (75.97) and **Tyler Soderstrom** (72.71) benefit significantly from favorable pitching matchups against Lowder and Littell, respectively; both hitters enjoy double-digit bumps from platoon advantages and park factors. **Vinnie Pasquantino** (72.92) also clears the 70-point threshold, as his matchup against Canning provides enough SP skill weakness to cement him as a high-floor utility play.\n\nThe optimizer\u2019s most difficult bench decision involved **Austin Riley** (52.16), who fell just shy of the starting threshold despite a solid base projection. While **Austin Riley** offers pedigree, the matchup against Gore\u2019s 3.90 xERA and the general efficiency of the starting infield\u2014particularly the platoon-advantaged **Isaac Paredes** (56.12) and **Josh Naylor** (58.67)\u2014squeezed him out of the lineup. Elsewhere, the squad must navigate the absence of **Jose Caballero**, who is confirmed for a rainout, and stay alert on the status of **Zach McKinstry** (50.11), who remains in the lineup based on a projected #9 slot against Rodriguez. While **Corbin Carroll** (69.43) faces a tough test against May, his elite speed profile remains non-negotiable in our 5x5 framework, even with a slight penalty for the opposing arm's hold potential.",
